                <pb facs="0490" n="405" sameAs="frus:frus1919v02/pg405"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72113/1054: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Honduras</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Jones</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tegucigalpa</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-06-26T18:00:00-05:48"><hi rendition="#italic">June 26, 1919,
                                6 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received June 28,
                            12.35 p.m.</hi>]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Your May 26, 6 p.m. I have just received a note from President Bertrand to the
                    effect that no enemy properties have been taken over up to the present time by
                    the Government of Honduras and that inasmuch as Germans here filed protest to
                    declare Honduranean alien property law unconstitutional nothing can be done
                    until the Supreme Court renders its decision. Nothing definite can be obtained
                    either from the President or Alien Property Custodian.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Jones</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
